According to tabs-343.txt,

A quarterstaff does d6 damage to 'small' enemies, and d6 damage to
'large' ones.
A short sword does d6 and d8 repectively.
Standard daggers: d4 and d3 (orcish daggers are slightly worse, elven
daggers slightly better)

(In case you're not familiar with the notation, 'd6' is a random roll
of a single six-sided die, 'd8' is an eight-sided die, etc.  '3d6'
would be *three* six-sided dice.)

Note that a wizard can only advance to basic skill with a short sword,
while she can advance to expert skill with quarterstaves.  Because a
higher skill level gives you both to-hit, and damage bonuses, and
because a wizard begins the game already at basic skill with staves, I
would *not* recommend ditching the staff for a sword.

I find that daggers can be very deadly weapons themselves.  For one
thing, all daggers have an intrinsic +2 to-hit bonus.  Another neat
thing is that, at higher skill levels, more daggers can be thrown in a
single turn.  A couple of rounds of this, and many enemies will be dead
before they even get close enough to touch you.  Since wizards can get
to  Expert level in daggers as well, they're a very popular and
succesful choice.

The to-hit and damage bonuses that advancing skills gives you are the
same for all melee weapons, so only comparing the base damage of the
weapons remains valid.

